I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the supplied refinement utilities first, then establish candidate phases and build the GSAS-II model with branch-safe checkpoints. I’ll keep structural identification and quantitative fractions conditional on full-pattern evidence and the required audits.

The chemical context is CaCO₃ + WO₃ heated at 500 °C, so Ca–W–O reaction products and residual precursors are plausible, but I won’t assume them. The next pass will extract the observed peak inventory and inspect the available indexing/database and GSAS-II interfaces so candidates are tested against the entire pattern.

Peak positions already separate into at least three coherent families: a monoclinic-WO₃-like triplet near 23–25°, a scheelite-like CaWO₄ family, and a calcite-like family including 29.71°, 43.47°, 47.52°, and 48.53°. I’m now testing the relevant polymorphs and alternate Ca–W–O structures rather than assigning those families from one peak apiece.

The candidate audits reject the malformed monoclinic WO₃ entry with 1.57 Å O–O contacts; a valid equivalent-setting P2₁/c model (COD 2311041) is available. Calcite and tetragonal scheelite models pass the occupancy/contact checks. I’m creating the first stable three-phase project now and will compare WO₃ symmetry and alternative Ca–W–O/CaCO₃ structures under the same nuisance model.

The first refinement exposed and fixed an exact scale degeneracy (the global histogram scale had been free alongside all phase scales). With that removed, the three-phase model is stable and already lowers Rwp from 18.93% to 15.12% before profile refinement; cell shifts are modest, and all three scales remain positive. I’m running the matched replacement and leave-one-out branches now, with stricter repeated convergence before judging them.

The converged minimal preferred branch reaches Rwp = 12.80% with positive conditional fractions of roughly 7.6% CaWO₄, 54.7% WO₃, and 37.7% calcite. The largest residuals sit on already modeled peak families, which points to profile/intensity-envelope refinement rather than an obvious missing phase; the alternative-symmetry branches are still running.

The WO₃ discrimination is now clear across the full range: P2₁/c gives 12.80% Rwp, while triclinic P‑1 stalls at 14.48% with poorer low/mid-angle behavior, Pnma gives 26.22%, and the small-cell P2₁/c polymorph gives 44.55%. The valid P2₁/c precursor structure is therefore strongly preferred; Ca–W–O and carbonate replacements are next.

All three phases have matched leave-one-out support: removing CaWO₄ raises Rwp to 16.77%, removing calcite to 18.00%, and removing WO₃ to 48.05%, with degradation across multiple angular regions. Monoclinic CaWO₄, Ca₃WO₆, and aragonite either collapse to zero scale or become unstable. The three-phase inventory is therefore fixed for profile-model selection; I’m now applying the required zero/displacement branch test from the stable baseline.

The angular test selects specimen displacement alone (−494 µm, Rwp = 12.753%); zero-only is slightly worse, while the nominally lower joint fit is rejected because zero and displacement correlate at 0.9997. Visual inspection shows phase-dependent peak widths—especially broader CaWO₄—so I’m screening global width terms and envelope alternatives before allowing phase-specific size/strain.

Global profile screening strongly favors a positive Lorentzian X term: it lowers Rwp from 12.753% to 9.845% with improvement in every angular region and no high parameter correlations. U and Y help less; V is rejected as 95.3% correlated with W. I’m finishing the polarization/roughness/asymmetry comparisons, then I’ll test phase size and strain from the best global branch.

CaWO₄ broadening is independently supported by both models: isotropic size gives the better fit (Rwp = 8.924%, about 46 nm) and isotropic microstrain gives 9.206% (about 10,400 µstrain), each improving multiple regions without correlations. I’ll carry both into a matched joint test only after checking WO₃ and calcite independently, as required.

After accepting CaWO₄ size broadening, both WO₃ size and strain become reproducibly supported; strain is the better single term (Rwp = 8.314%, ~3,900 µstrain), while calcite size runs to the upper bound and calcite strain becomes negative, so both calcite terms are rejected. The CaWO₄ size+strain joint branch also yields negative strain and is rejected. I’m now testing WO₃ size+strain jointly and then rechecking global/envelope terms from that mature state.

The mature envelope comparison now changes the conclusion: positive Suortti roughness B is best (Rwp = 7.828%, improvement in all five regions), outperforming refined polarization (8.045%). Extra U/V/Y terms give only tiny gains with negative coefficients; axial divergence is 98.2% correlated with displacement. I’m retaining only roughness B and screening simple March–Dollase axes phase-by-phase.

The texture branches have finished. I’m checking whether any orientation term improves several independent reflection regions enough to justify the added parameter; then I’ll lock the nuisance model before refining atomic coordinates.

Calcite shows the only defensible orientation effect: a mild [001] March–Dollase term improves the 25–40°, 60–80°, and 80–100° regions (Rwp 7.828→7.744%, coefficient 0.918). The other axes give only marginal or localized gains, so I’m carrying just this one term into structural-coordinate tests.

The first structural test is informative: calcite’s oxygen shift is negligible, and unconstrained WO₃ tungsten coordinates lower Rwp substantially but oscillate with a 98.7% correlation between the two W y-displacements, so that branch is not acceptable. I’m testing the chemically natural antisymmetric W displacement mode that removes that degeneracy, while auditing the small scheelite oxygen shift for geometry.

The antisymmetric WO₃ W mode converged cleanly and passed the geometry audit, lowering Rwp from 7.744% to 7.446% with benefits across 25–100°. I’m now checking whether the weaker scheelite/calcite coordinate adjustments remain supported once that dominant structural correction is present.

The WO₃ oxygen-coordinate branch is nonconvergent, and grouped ADPs refine to an unphysical negative oxygen value with a 96% scale/envelope correlation; both are rejected. Since the source CIF omitted WO₃ displacement factors entirely, I’ll use small fixed positive room-temperature values (W 0.005, O 0.010 Å²) and then re-test the competing envelope/profile terms.

The angular re-test is still running through matched zero-only, displacement-only, and joint branches. This is the expected longer pass because each branch restores cells, scales, background, profile, microstructure, and texture in stages before comparison.

The angular comparison retained displacement-only; the joint correction was rejected at 99.97% Zero/Shift correlation. I’m now repeating the global profile/envelope screen against the updated structures and fixed positive WO₃ ADPs, including Gaussian/Lorentzian alternatives, polarization, axial divergence, and roughness.

The re-screen is confirming that most added profile terms are either negligible or correlated; axial divergence is especially correlated with displacement and is rejected. The remaining question is whether CaWO₄ size and WO₃ strain are each independently supported after the structural update, which the current matched branches are testing.

I found an important modeling detail: GSAS-II’s fixed default size/strain values are not mathematically zero. I rebuilt a true no-microstructure baseline (10 μm size, 1 με strain) and am rerunning all six independent phase/term tests from that common state, so retained broadening will be genuinely evidence-based.

From the corrected blank baseline, CaWO₄ size (about 49 nm) and CaWO₄ strain each independently improve the complete pattern; WO₃ size and calcite terms do not. I’m now adding candidates one at a time after retaining the stronger CaWO₄ size term, as required before considering any joint broadening model.

The clean microstructure sequence now supports a parsimonious model: CaWO₄ size ≈44 nm plus ≈4300 με strain, and WO₃ ≈4265 με strain. WO₃ size collapses to the no-effect limit; calcite broadening is not independently supported. I’m doing the required final profile/envelope and texture pass from this corrected state.

The final profile pass exposed an exact competing explanation: a positive global Lorentzian \(Y\) term gives the same fit as assigning a common strain offset to calcite and shifting the other phase strains. Because calcite strain failed its independent test, I’m carrying the smaller global-\(Y\) model and will explicitly report that absolute microstrain values are instrument-model dependent.

No additional texture term survives: the best WO₃ axis is only 0.75% from random and trades a clear low-angle worsening for mid-angle gains. I’m retaining only the mild calcite [001] term and now performing matched leave-one-out tests on every optional term in the proposed final model.

Every retained optional term degrades the matched fit when removed; the largest penalties are roughness, WO₃ strain, instrument \(X\), and CaWO₄ size. The mature structural-alternative retest is now running—same complete nuisance model, one replacement at a time—so candidate rejection will not depend on the earlier coarse profile.

The replacement pass is taking longer because several incorrect cells require repeated damped cycles before they can be rejected cleanly. The source project remains untouched; each branch started from its own saved mature baseline and will be judged on convergence, positive scale, and full-range residuals.

One WO₃ alternative exposed an importer defect: its CIF uses charged type tokens (`W+`, `O-`), which GSAS-II silently converted to hydrogen. I’m treating that imported branch as malformed, making a charge-neutral token-only copy (`W`, `O`), auditing it, and rerunning the replacement so the cell family gets a fair test.

The only positive fourth-phase scale was trace aragonite (~0.74 wt%, 3.7σ), but all of its strongest lines overlap existing WO₃/calcite reflections and most χ² gain occurs outside those peaks. It fails the required multi-unique-peak test, so I’m not reporting it as a phase; the three-phase fractions remain conditional on modeled crystalline material.

The final angular replay again retains displacement-only; the lower-Rwp joint branch is rejected at 99.97% correlation. The phase/profile model is now frozen. I’m generating the final GPX, CIFs, profile table, normalized fractions, and audit-ready report from that exact saved state.

The required artifacts are written. The final GPX passes convergence, profile regeneration, and positive total-width checks; all three CIFs pass composition/occupancy/contact audits. I’m doing the last cross-file readback now, including recomputing fractions from `final.gpx` and checking every `fit.csv` value.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Monoclinic γ-WO₃, P 21/c | 56.81 ± 0.16 wt% |
| Calcite CaCO₃, R -3 c | 30.88 ± 0.20 wt% |
| Scheelite CaWO₄, I 41/a | 12.30 ± 0.11 wt% |

Final fit: Rwp 7.451%, Rp 5.426%, GOF 3.118. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material only. Trace aragonite remains unconfirmed because its possible contribution lacks unique-peak support.

